## Overview

This lesson focuses on using JavaScript to interact with web APIs. I learned how to retrieve, create, and manage data using the Fetch API and asynchronous programming techniques such as Promises and async/await.

The exercises demonstrate how modern web applications communicate with servers while handling loading states and errors gracefully.

---

## Topics Covered

- Fetch API
- GET Requests
- POST Requests
- Async/Await
- Error Handling
- Loading States
- JSON Data
- Search and Filter

---

## Files Included

### fetch.js

This file demonstrates how to:

- Fetch data from a public API
- Display user information
- Parse JSON data
- Use async/await with the Fetch API

---

### error-handling.js

This file demonstrates how to:

- Handle network errors
- Check API response status
- Display meaningful error messages
- Use try...catch with asynchronous code

---

### loading-state.js

This file demonstrates how to:

- Show a loading message while data is being fetched
- Hide the loading message after the request completes
- Display the fetched data
- Improve the user experience during asynchronous operations

---

### post-request.js *(Optional Enhancement)*

This file demonstrates how to:

- Send data to an API using a POST request
- Create a new resource
- Convert JavaScript objects to JSON
- Display the API response

---

### search-filter.js *(Optional Enhancement)*

This file demonstrates how to:

- Search users by name
- Search users by email
- Sort users alphabetically
- Filter users by city

---

## Learning Outcomes

After completing this lesson, I can:

- Use the Fetch API to retrieve data from an API.
- Work with JSON data.
- Use async/await with API requests.
- Handle errors using try...catch.
- Display loading states while waiting for data.
- Send POST requests to create new resources.
- Build search and filtering functionality for API data.

---

## APIs Used

This project uses the free JSONPlaceholder API for practice:

- https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users
- https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts

The Weather Dashboard project uses the OpenWeatherMap API to display real-time weather information.

